## 5. Behind the Scenes: How React Updates the UI

**The core rule of React:** whenever a state variable updates, React re-renders the component.

### Real DOM vs. Virtual DOM

| Feature          | Real DOM                                          | Virtual DOM                                        |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Nature           | Actual browser DOM elements (`<div>`, `<button>`) | In-memory representation made of plain JS objects  |
| Performance cost | Updating/repainting is slow and heavy             | Creating & comparing JS objects is extremely fast  |
| Updates          | Direct browser DOM manipulation                   | Calculated via diffing, then targeted DOM patching |

### React Elements & Functional Components

When you write JSX like `<RestaurantCard />` or `<div className="card">`, React converts it into a **React Element** via `React.createElement(...)`.

A React Element is just a lightweight JS object:

```js
{
  type: 'div',
  props: {
    className: 'card',
    children: 'Dominos'
  }
}
```

The tree of all these nested JS objects makes up the **Virtual DOM**.

### Reconciliation & the Diff Algorithm

- **Reconciliation:** the process by which React syncs the in-memory Virtual DOM with the real browser DOM.
- **Diff algorithm:** compares the previous Virtual DOM tree with the newly generated tree to compute the exact differences.

**Practical example (7 cards → 3 cards):**

1. Initial render: 7 restaurant cards → Virtual DOM Tree A has 7 nodes.
2. Filter applied: state updates to 3 restaurants → Virtual DOM Tree B has 3 nodes.
3. Diffing: React compares Tree A and Tree B, identifies that 4 specific card nodes are no longer present.
4. Targeted update: React removes only those 4 nodes from the real DOM, rather than re-rendering the whole container or reloading the page.

### Conceptual Diff Algorithm (JS)

```js
// 1. Virtual DOM trees represented as plain JavaScript objects
const oldVNode = {
  type: "div",
  props: { className: "res-container" },
  children: [
    { type: "div", props: { id: "card-1" }, children: ["Dominos"] },
    { type: "div", props: { id: "card-2" }, children: ["KFC"] },
    { type: "div", props: { id: "card-3" }, children: ["Subway"] },
  ],
};

const newVNode = {
  type: "div",
  props: { className: "res-container" },
  children: [
    { type: "div", props: { id: "card-1" }, children: ["Dominos"] },
    { type: "div", props: { id: "card-2" }, children: ["KFC"] },
  ],
};

// 2. Simplified diff function
function diff(oldTree, newTree) {
  // Case 1: node was removed
  if (!newTree) {
    return { type: "REMOVE_NODE" };
  }

  // Case 2: node was created
  if (!oldTree) {
    return { type: "CREATE_NODE", newTree };
  }

  // Case 3: node tag or text changed
  if (
    typeof oldTree !== typeof newTree ||
    (typeof oldTree === "string" && oldTree !== newTree) ||
    oldTree.type !== newTree.type
  ) {
    return { type: "REPLACE_NODE", newTree };
  }

  // Case 4: same element -> compare children recursively
  const childPatches = [];
  const maxChildren = Math.max(
    oldTree.children?.length || 0,
    newTree.children?.length || 0,
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < maxChildren; i++) {
    childPatches.push(diff(oldTree.children?.[i], newTree.children?.[i]));
  }

  return {
    type: "UPDATE_CHILDREN",
    childPatches,
  };
}

// Compute patch in memory
const patch = diff(oldVNode, newVNode);
console.log(patch);
```

