## 6. React 16 & React Fiber

In React 16, the core reconciliation engine was completely rewritten and named **React Fiber**.

**Why React Fiber?**

- The prior reconciliation engine (Stack Reconciler) was synchronous and could block the main UI thread during heavy computations.
- Fiber introduces **incremental rendering**: the ability to pause, prioritize, split, and resume rendering work in small chunks.
- This keeps animations, input typing, and button clicks responsive and smooth even during large state changes.

---

## 7. Quick Summary Cheat Sheet

| Concept         | What it is / does                                                                         |
| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| React Hook      | A normal JavaScript utility function provided by React                                    |
| `useState()`    | Returns `[stateVar, setStateVar]`; tracks reactive state and triggers re-render on change |
| State re-render | Changing state causes the component function to re-execute and return fresh JSX           |
| Virtual DOM     | Lightweight JS object representation of the real HTML DOM                                 |
| Reconciliation  | The process of diffing Virtual DOM trees and updating only changed real DOM nodes         |
| React Fiber     | React 16's reconciliation engine enabling fast, incremental, prioritized rendering        |
